WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.300
Even if the expanded universe has taken the
time to answer some burning questions, the

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:10.390
Star Wars films are absolutely packed with
fascinating mysteries, some of which may never

00:00:10.390 --> 00:00:11.440
be addressed again.

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:15.940
Here are just some of the biggest questions
Star Wars has yet to answer.

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:22.740
The prequel trilogy is all about charting
the rise and fall of Anakin Skywalker from

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:27.040
boy with tons of potential to hotshot Jedi
to Dark Lord of the Sith.

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:33.340
That all begins with Qui-Gon Jinn's realization
that Anakin, whose midichlorian ratings and

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:37.260
natural talents seem off the charts, must
be the Chosen One.

00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:42.780
When he relays his suspicions to the Jedi
Council, Mace Windu brings up, quote, "the

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:45.620
prophecy of the one who will bring balance
to the Force."

00:00:46.109 --> 00:00:52.480
Yoda, Mace, Qui-Gon, and Obi-Wan Kenobi are
all very aware of this prophecy, and Anakin's

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:57.160
Chosen One narrative continues right up to
the moment he loses his lightsaber duel with

00:00:57.160 --> 00:00:59.400
Obi-Wan in Revenge of the Sith.

00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:04.740
So, the prequels are very clear that a prophecy
exists and that it's well-known among Jedi

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:09.800
Masters, but what the films don't make clear
is exactly where the prophecy comes from.

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:11.720
Who first articulated it?

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:13.520
How long has it been around?

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:18.260
Does every Jedi take it seriously or do a
lot of them believe it's made-up hogwash that

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:21.520
has no practical application when it comes
to protecting the galaxy?

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:26.840
And what does "bring balance to the Force"
even mean when the Jedi believe that they've

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:28.820
long-since defeated the Sith?

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:35.100
The original trilogy makes it a bit of a point
to never explain much about Yoda, the enigmatic

00:01:35.110 --> 00:01:40.430
Jedi Master who lives on the swamp planet
of Dagobah and even pretends he's not who

00:01:40.430 --> 00:01:45.410
he really is when he first meets Luke Skywalker,
just to teach the young apprentice a lesson

00:01:45.410 --> 00:01:47.360
in expectation versus reality.

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:48.640
"I'm looking for a great warrior."

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:49.740
"Oh!

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:52.100
Great warrior!

00:01:55.540 --> 00:01:56.920
Wars not make one great."

00:01:57.080 --> 00:02:01.180
All we really know about the little green
guy in those original movies is that he's

00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:04.820
a Jedi Master, he taught Obi-Wan, and he can
teach Luke.

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:10.520
The mystery surrounding Yoda, combined with
his unique appearance, led many fans to wonder

00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:16.340
what the master's backstory really was, and
in particular if the alien species he belonged

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:17.980
to would ever be revealed.

00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:23.300
Then the prequels came along, and while we
certainly learned more about Yoda's day job

00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:27.340
as the head of the Jedi Council, we still
didn't learn much about his background.

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:32.970
The mystery only deepened when The Phantom
Menace introduced a second Jedi Master, Yaddle,

00:02:32.970 --> 00:02:38.300
who seemed to be of the exact same species,
teasing some kind of connection that might

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:39.560
pay off in a future film.

00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:44.830
It didn't, of course, and while there are
certain supplemental stories you can dig up

00:02:44.830 --> 00:02:50.180
out there, for many fans Yoda remains a mysterious
figure, and that's just the way they like

00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:50.680
it.

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:56.200
The Star Wars films are full of famous death
scenes that might not always be as final as

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:56.700
they seem.

00:02:56.920 --> 00:03:01.740
The second film in the saga reveals that even
after Obi-Wan Kenobi dies, he's able to come

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:06.140
back as a Force ghost, something Yoda is also
later able to do.

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:11.190
The prequels have taken this a step further,
with Solo: A Star Wars Story carrying on the

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:16.590
story told in The Clone Wars TV series which
revealed that the Sith Lord Darth Maul did

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:21.670
not die when he was cut in half and thrown
down a shaft by Obi-Wan Kenobi in The Phantom

00:03:21.670 --> 00:03:22.160
Menace.

00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:27.900
He somehow survived that fall and managed
to get himself a sweet new pair of legs.

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:33.920
But surely that can't be true for other Force-using
warriors who fell in the prequels…right?

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:36.300
As it turns out, maybe it can.

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:42.020
In 2016, while answering fan questions via
Twitter, Samuel L. Jackson declared:

00:03:42.460 --> 00:03:45.540
"In my mind, [Mace Windu is] not dead."

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:51.320
He justified his theory by citing the Jedi
ability to fall from great distances and land

00:03:51.320 --> 00:03:51.820
safely.

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:56.420
After all, we don't really see him dying in
Revenge of the Sith, which does raise some

00:03:56.420 --> 00:04:00.860
questions when compared to all the other scenes
in which we clearly watch Jedi getting cut

00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:01.360
down.

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:06.900
Speaking to Entertainment Weekly later, Jackson
even said that he'd gotten his theory co-signed

00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:07.980
by George Lucas.

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:09.520
He told EW:

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:11.620
"George is like 'I'm OK with that.

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:12.780
You can be alive.'"

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:18.819
So, if you believe Jackson, Mace Windu is
still out there having one-handed Jedi adventures

00:04:18.820 --> 00:04:19.480
somewhere.

00:04:19.820 --> 00:04:21.979
What other Jedi might have made it out alive?

00:04:22.979 --> 00:04:28.539
The first time we see a Jedi Master die in
A New Hope, Obi-Wan Kenobi simply vanishes

00:04:28.540 --> 00:04:31.460
when Darth Vader swings a lightsaber through
his body.

00:04:31.960 --> 00:04:37.599
The second time we see a Jedi Master die in
Return of the Jedi, Yoda quietly disappears

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:38.620
from his sickbed.

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:44.440
Both Jedi leave a pile of clothes in their
wake but nothing else, something Luke Skywalker

00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:46.820
also did when he perished in The Last Jedi.

00:04:47.449 --> 00:04:52.199
For a significant amount of time in Star Wars
lore, you could have been forgiven for simply

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:57.000
thinking that Jedi Masters vanish when they
die, but that changed with The Phantom Menace,

00:04:57.260 --> 00:05:02.669
when Qui-Gon Jinn's body remained in place
after his death, as did the bodies of numerous

00:05:02.669 --> 00:05:05.259
other Jedi during the Purge in Revenge of
the Sith.

00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:08.380
So, why do some Jedi vanish and others don't?

00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:10.560
Is it based on their Force level?

00:05:10.780 --> 00:05:12.220
Do they train themselves to do that?

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:17.259
Or is it simply that some choose to remove
their own body from the mortal plane while

00:05:17.260 --> 00:05:18.540
others don't care?

00:05:19.160 --> 00:05:23.560
The movies never explore this apparent inconsistency,
but they almost did.

00:05:23.780 --> 00:05:27.599
Revenge of the Sith was set to reveal that
the vanishing act had something to do with

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:32.040
a discovery that Qui-Gon had made in the afterlife,
but the scene was cut.

00:05:32.840 --> 00:05:37.780
The longer you sit with the deeds and words
of Obi-Wan Kenobi in the original Star Wars

00:05:37.780 --> 00:05:41.380
trilogy, the more frustrating some of his
various decisions become.

00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:47.960
Yes, Obi-Wan saved Luke in a moment of crisis,
gave him his heirloom lightsaber, and started

00:05:47.960 --> 00:05:49.740
to teach him the ways of the Force.

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:55.319
But he also seems to have been determined
to obscure the truth as much as possible,

00:05:55.320 --> 00:05:57.900
particularly when it came to who Luke's father
was.

00:05:58.180 --> 00:06:03.000
Obi-Wan obviously could have chosen to spill
the beans right up front that Luke's father

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:08.119
was Darth Vader, but held off on that and
instead said that Vader had betrayed and killed

00:06:08.120 --> 00:06:08.960
ol' papadukes.

00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:13.960
Things get more complex when you start to
factor in what Obi-Wan knew thanks to the

00:06:13.961 --> 00:06:18.430
events of the prequels, which reveal that
he was not only there when Anakin Skywalker

00:06:18.430 --> 00:06:24.059
turned into Darth Vader, but was also there
when Luke and Leia were born, and there when

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:26.509
the plan to separate and hide them was hatched.

00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:28.760
He also hung out a lot with R2-D2.

00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:34.000
That Obi-Wan doesn't seem to acknowledge that
Leia is Luke's sister or ever acknowledge

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:38.520
R2-D2 as an old friend just adds another layer
of confusion to his thinking.

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:42.820
There are reasons for some of it now, but
there's a lot of head-scratching going on

00:06:42.820 --> 00:06:43.320
here.

00:06:43.840 --> 00:06:49.499
Perhaps no other character in all of the main
Star Wars saga has been present for as many

00:06:49.500 --> 00:06:51.820
historic moments as R2-D2.

00:06:52.040 --> 00:06:57.580
Well, maybe C-3PO can compete with him, but
unlike Artoo, Threepio had his mind wiped

00:06:57.580 --> 00:07:02.480
at the end of Revenge of the Sith, so he can't
very well look at Darth Vader and realize,

00:07:02.600 --> 00:07:04.200
"Oh dear, that's my maker."

00:07:04.460 --> 00:07:10.219
Artoo, on the other hand, has a memory packed
with fascinating moments and galaxy-altering

00:07:10.219 --> 00:07:14.560
events, from the birth of the Skywalker twins
to the turn of Anakin to the whole of the

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:15.360
Clone Wars.

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:20.860
He was there for all of it, and as far as
we know from the films, he hasn't forgotten

00:07:20.860 --> 00:07:21.360
any of it.

00:07:21.960 --> 00:07:23.380
So why doesn't he speak up?

00:07:23.900 --> 00:07:28.619
Seriously, the prequels retroactively add
a ton of moments to the original trilogy where

00:07:28.619 --> 00:07:33.251
Artoo could have simply said, "Hey, she's
your sister," or, "Hey, remember me?

00:07:33.260 --> 00:07:35.520
We hung out together during the Clone Wars."

00:07:35.640 --> 00:07:39.940
Even "Hey, that guy built you, Threepio" would
have been relevant.

00:07:40.220 --> 00:07:43.660
Instead, he remains silent for reasons that
are unknown to us.

00:07:44.220 --> 00:07:48.969
All we have are theories and a hope that perhaps
one day Artoo will break down and explain

00:07:48.969 --> 00:07:49.460
himself.

00:07:49.860 --> 00:07:55.599
At the end of Revenge of the Sith, Bail Organa,
Obi-Wan Kenobi, and Yoda have a conversation

00:07:55.600 --> 00:07:59.340
about the future of the Skywalker twins now
that their mother, has passed away.

00:07:59.949 --> 00:08:04.760
Obi-Wan says that the children should be hidden
where the Sith cannot sense them, and Yoda

00:08:04.760 --> 00:08:09.060
declares that it's better to split them up
and hide their relation to one another.

00:08:09.349 --> 00:08:15.240
Obi-Wan takes Luke to Tatooine to live with
his aunt and uncle, while Leia goes with Bail

00:08:15.240 --> 00:08:16.820
Organa to Alderaan.

00:08:17.149 --> 00:08:22.340
Roughly two decades later, during The Empire
Strikes Back, Darth Vader begins to understand

00:08:22.349 --> 00:08:27.349
that he is in the presence of his long-lost
son, and eventually tells Luke that he's his

00:08:27.349 --> 00:08:27.840
father.

00:08:28.140 --> 00:08:32.219
It makes sense that he might not have known
it until then, particularly if he never made

00:08:32.220 --> 00:08:35.660
time in his busy Empire-building schedule
to head back to Tatooine.

00:08:36.220 --> 00:08:37.220
But what about Leia?

00:08:37.560 --> 00:08:41.560
When she and Vader first cross paths in A
New Hope, it's clear that they've known each

00:08:41.560 --> 00:08:44.620
other for some time, and he's aware of her
skills as a leader.

00:08:45.320 --> 00:08:48.339
Why doesn't he ever seem to sense that she's
his daughter?

00:08:48.740 --> 00:08:52.120
Is she somehow holding him off with her own
limited use of the Force?

00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:56.680
Is he just dismissive of the very idea of
it because she grew up so close to him?

00:08:56.980 --> 00:09:02.020
The films never make it clear, but Vader definitely
had a closer connection with one twin than

00:09:02.020 --> 00:09:02.540
the other.

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:07.860
The sequel trilogy decided to keep up the
Star Wars tradition of a shadowy figure pulling

00:09:07.870 --> 00:09:13.690
the strings of darkness by introducing Supreme
Leader Snoke, a humanoid alien who, in The

00:09:13.690 --> 00:09:17.500
Force Awakens, only appears in the form of
a giant hologram.

00:09:18.040 --> 00:09:20.680
That film gave us very little to go on about
Snoke.

00:09:21.240 --> 00:09:26.000
All we really knew was the he led the First
Order, he trained Kylo Ren, and he wanted

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:30.019
to destroy Luke Skywalker and, by extension,
the Jedi.

00:09:30.220 --> 00:09:35.100
Though certain supplemental materials have
since shed a little more light on Snoke, even

00:09:35.100 --> 00:09:40.120
The Last Jedi didn't give us much more information
before unceremoniously slicing the Supreme

00:09:40.120 --> 00:09:43.120
Leader in half and ending his reign over the
First Order.

00:09:43.360 --> 00:09:48.610
It seems possible that the upcoming Rise of
Skywalker will offer more details about him,

00:09:48.610 --> 00:09:52.779
but it's equally possible that the film will
be so concerned with moving forward that it

00:09:52.780 --> 00:09:56.529
won't spend enough time looking back to tell
us anything new about Snoke.

00:09:56.700 --> 00:09:57.860
So, who was he?

00:09:58.300 --> 00:10:01.220
Why was he a Dark Side Force user but not
a Sith?

00:10:01.580 --> 00:10:03.760
Why was he obsessed with the Skywalkers?

00:10:04.160 --> 00:10:08.360
How did he carve out the First Order from
the remnants of the Empire in such a relatively

00:10:08.360 --> 00:10:09.540
brief period of time?

00:10:10.200 --> 00:10:14.500
The answers to these questions, like so many
others, might very well be lost to the void.

00:10:15.200 --> 00:10:19.500
In Star Wars: The Force Awakens we finally
learn what happened to Luke Skywalker's first

00:10:19.500 --> 00:10:24.399
lightsaber, the one passed down to him by
Obi-Wan Kenobi from his father, which he lost

00:10:24.400 --> 00:10:26.600
while dueling Darth Vader in Cloud City.

00:10:27.220 --> 00:10:29.800
Or at least, we learn part of what happened
to it.

00:10:30.380 --> 00:10:36.790
The lightsaber was "locked away" in the possession
of Maz Kanata at her castle on Takodana, but

00:10:36.790 --> 00:10:41.900
when Han Solo asks where she got it, Maz only
answers that she's had it, quote, "for ages.”

00:10:42.340 --> 00:10:46.780
The matter of how it came to be in her possession
is left equally mysterious.

00:10:46.920 --> 00:10:51.940
In the context of The Force Awakens, Maz shrugs
this question off because the First Order

00:10:51.940 --> 00:10:56.320
is attacking and the movie can't slow down
to hear a big information dump about the lineage

00:10:56.320 --> 00:10:57.180
of a laser sword.

00:10:57.760 --> 00:11:02.500
But that hasn't stopped fans from wondering
exactly how Maz ended up with the lightsaber.

00:11:02.820 --> 00:11:09.640
Luke dropped it, along with his hand, on Bespin
more than 30 years before, so how long are

00:11:09.649 --> 00:11:12.270
the "ages" in which it's been in Maz's possession?

00:11:12.270 --> 00:11:14.120
Why didn't she tell Han about it before?

00:11:14.290 --> 00:11:19.080
Did she have it even before Luke vanished,
and if so, why didn't she try to give it back

00:11:19.080 --> 00:11:19.580
to him?

00:11:19.920 --> 00:11:24.360
Perhaps The Rise of Skywalker will give us
answers, but it seems quite possible that

00:11:24.360 --> 00:11:27.020
the film will be too busy with other plot
points to touch on it.

